{"cases":[{"attack":true,"case":"The target's claim that the preference is defensible because it claims 'no justification' for the psychological expectation contradicts Popper's own requirement for rational action. Popper argues we prefer better-corroborated theories not merely due to an unavoidable psychological habit (which would be irrational arbitrariness), but because a theory that has withstood severe tests is a better candidate for truth in the sense of having higher verisimilitude (truth-likeness), even if its probability remains zero. By reducing the preference to a mere 'psychological expectation' we 'cannot shed,' the target fails to make the strongest case for defensibility on Popper's terms; it instead concedes that the preference is non-rational (a brute fact of human psychology), which leaves the door open for the accusation that it smuggles in induction as an unjustified instinct rather than defending it as a critical, rational choice based on past performance without assuming future uniformity.","premise":"Popper distinguishes between irrational psychological habits and rational critical preferences based on verisimilitude.","premises_essential":["a2d5c68d648eb90f8df352e87ec5cbe5af4d1a09b39a320201a8390e7f7558f4"],"successor_question":"Does reducing the preference for corroborated theories to a mere 'psychological expectation' fail to capture Popper's argument for verisimilitude as a rational basis for action?","target":"a2d5c68d648eb90f8df352e87ec5cbe5af4d1a09b39a320201a8390e7f7558f4"}]}